\begin_layout Abstract
Bioconductor provides standardized data structures and statistical methods for high-throughput genomic analysis in R,
 whereas Python has become a primary environment for machine learning,
 image analysis,
 and general-purpose data science.
 Combining tools across both ecosystems often requires ad-hoc file conversions or embedded language runtimes that can lose metadata,
 introduce maintenance overhead,
 or lead to subtle differences in downstream results.
 Here,
 we present BiocPy,
 a collection of Python packages that support cross-language genomic workflows through three complementary layers:
 (1) native Python implementations of Bioconductor's core data structures;
 (2) lossless data exchange via direct reading and writing of serialized R (
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
.rds
\end_layout

\end_inset

) objects and the language-neutral 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ArtifactDB
\end_layout

\end_inset

 format for persistent,
 multi-stage pipelines;
 and (3) a cross-platform method development paradigm built on shared C++ libraries wrapped across R,
 Python,
 and WebAssembly to support identical statistical analyses across environments.
 All packages in the BiocPy ecosystem are published to the Python Package Index (PyPI) with source code hosted at 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "https://github.com/BiocPy"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

