Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bioinformatics sits at the intersection of molecular biology,
 statistics,
 and computer science.
 Bioconductor
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gentleman_bioconductor_2004,huber_orchestrating_2015"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 built upon the R programming language,
 was designed with a focus on statistical analysis and visualization of genomic data.
 Over the past two decades,
 it has grown into a repository of over 2,000 packages organized around shared data structures and design principles.
 Classes such as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lawrence_software_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "martin_morgan_summarizedexperiment_2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 serve as a common currency for data exchange,
 allowing developers to build and compose methods without defining bespoke representations for each package.
 Bioconductor remains a standard environment for statistical testing and established genomic workflows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Over the same period,
 Python has become the primary language for general-purpose data science and machine learning (ML).
 Supported by libraries such as NumPy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "harris_array_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 PyTorch
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "paszke_pytorch_2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and TensorFlow
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "abadi_tensorflow_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Python offers broad integration with modern computing infrastructure,
 including distributed computing and GPU acceleration.
 In genomics,
 Python adoption has grown substantially with single-cell analysis frameworks such as scverse
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "virshup_scverse_2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and GPU-accelerated toolkits such as rapids-singlecell
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dicks_scverserapids_singlecell_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In practice,
 genomic analyses frequently span both ecosystems:
 an analyst may rely on Bioconductor packages for experimental design,
 normalization,
 or differential expression,
 and then move to Python for deep learning or large-scale integration.
 Bridging these steps typically relies on exporting intermediate flat files,
 which strips nested metadata and requires manual reassembly,
 or reimplementing statistical routines in Python,
 which can introduce subtle algorithmic discrepancies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy addresses this gap by implementing Bioconductor’s core class definitions directly in Python.
 Rather than embedding an R session inside Python or proposing a new data standard,
 BiocPy provides native Python classes such as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 that mirror the structure and behavior of their R counterparts.
 Keeping the conceptual data model consistent reduces friction when moving data or translating workflows between languages,
 while still allowing direct conversion to standard Python structures when needed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The BiocPy framework is targeted at developers who require the reliability of Bioconductor’s data structures within a Python environment.
 Unlike rpy2
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_rpy2rpy2_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which embeds an R runtime and can complicate deployments,
 BiocPy is lightweight and native to Python.
 Similarly,
 while 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
AnnData
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "virshup_anndata_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is well-suited for single-cell expression matrices,
 it lacks the broader generality of 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
MultiAssayExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and does not natively support genomic interval coordinates (such as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset

) or range-based operations.
 BiocPy supports this broader spectrum of genomic data types while providing converters to and from 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
pandas
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
AnnData
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
MuData
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Core Data Structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy adapts Bioconductor’s S4 design principles to idiomatic Python rather than simply translating the R class definitions.
 The goal is not to replace existing Python scientific libraries,
 but to provide containers that maintain structural and semantic equivalence with Bioconductor objects.
 Although BiocPy classes provide methods to convert to and from popular Python representations such as pandas 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DataFrame
\end_layout

\end_inset

s
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "mckinney_pandas_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
AnnData
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 those representations do not always capture nested metadata,
 genomic range coordinates,
 or sample-level mappings without loss of information.
 BiocPy containers preserve these members so that objects can move between R and Python without losing structural fidelity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A central design difference between R and Python is object mutability.
 R uses copy-on-write semantics,
 which prevents modifications to an object inside a function from unexpectedly mutating references held elsewhere.
 Python passes object references by default;
 in-place modifications to a shared metadata table or coordinate vector can silently corrupt parent containers or bypass dimensional validation.
 To prevent unintended side effects in multi-step pipelines,
 BiocPy encourages a functional programming style:
 setter and transformation methods (e.g.,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
set_column()
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
set_row_names()
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
set_assay()
\end_layout

\end_inset

) default to 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
in_place=False
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 returning a new shallow copy that shares unmodified underlying arrays while updating only the affected member.
 For interactive convenience,
 standard Python property assignments and item setters are also supported as aliases for in-place updates (
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
in_place=True
\end_layout

\end_inset

),
 which still trigger container validation checks.
\end_layout

