Containers for Experimental Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To organize high-throughput experimental assays alongside feature and sample annotations,
 BiocPy implements a hierarchy of experimental containers that mirror Bioconductor's S4 classes (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:MAE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 Every container validates dimensional consistency on construction and modification,
 ensuring that subsetting rows or columns remains synchronized across all assay matrices,
 feature annotations,
 and sample metadata.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
SummarizedExperiment (SE) & RangedSummarizedExperiment (RSE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_biocpysummarizedexperiment_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 serves as the base container for rectangular experimental data,
 where rows correspond to features (such as genes,
 transcripts,
 or genomic regions) and columns correspond to samples or cells.
 An SE instance stores one or more identically shaped 2D matrices in its 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
assays
\end_layout

\end_inset

 dictionary (supporting dense NumPy arrays,
 SciPy sparse matrices,
 or 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DelayedArray
\end_layout

\end_inset

 objects),
 paired with 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
row_data
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
column_data
\end_layout

\end_inset

 members represented as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
BiocFrame
\end_layout

\end_inset

 objects.
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
