\begin_layout Section
Cross-Language Data and Methods Interoperability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Providing compatible in-memory classes in Python is only part of cross-language integration.
 Workflows also require reliable ways to exchange serialized data on disk and guarantee that statistical algorithms behave consistently across languages.
 BiocPy addresses data exchange through two complementary mechanisms,
 direct reading and writing of R serialized (
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
.rds
\end_layout

\end_inset

) files via 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
rds2py
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and schema-validated,
 language-agnostic persistence via 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ArtifactDB
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and addresses algorithmic consistency through shared C++ libraries wrapped across R,
 Python,
 and WebAssembly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Reading and Writing Serialized RDS Files
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Many public genomic datasets and existing R pipelines rely on 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
.rds
\end_layout

\end_inset

 files created via 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
saveRDS()
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 which cannot be read or written natively in Python.
 To support direct 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
.rds
\end_layout

\end_inset

 exchange without requiring an R installation,
 BiocPy includes 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
rds2py
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_biocpyrds2py_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 a library for reading and writing R’s binary serialization format.
 When reading 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
.rds
\end_layout

\end_inset

 files,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
rds2py
\end_layout

\end_inset

 parses the low-level R object representation from disk into a lightweight dictionary structure and dispatches registered constructors to instantiate the corresponding BiocPy class,
 parsing atomic vectors and sparse matrix buffers directly into NumPy arrays and SciPy sparse matrices without requiring an intermediate R session.
 Conversely,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
rds2py
\end_layout

\end_inset

 can serialize Python data structures and BiocPy containers back into standard 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
.rds
\end_layout

\end_inset

 files that can be loaded directly in R via 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
readRDS()
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 allowing Python tools to integrate into existing R/Bioconductor workflows without changing file formats (see the executable 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "code snippet"
target "https://github.com/biocpy/paper/blob/main/snippets/rds2py_example.py"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Language-Agnostic Formats with ArtifactDB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
rds2py
\end_layout

\end_inset

 provides convenient 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
.rds
\end_layout

\end_inset

 exchange with existing R scripts,
 R's binary serialization format requires reading or writing entire objects in memory and is tied to R's internal object representation rather than open storage standards.
 For language-agnostic archival storage and out-of-core access to large matrices,
 BiocPy integrates with the schemas and specifications defined by ArtifactDB
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_artifactdb_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 implemented by the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
alabaster
\end_layout

\end_inset

 suite in R
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_artifactdbalabasterbase_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
dolomite
\end_layout

\end_inset

 suite in Python
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_artifactdbdolomite-base_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under this framework,
 complex Bioconductor and BiocPy objects are decomposed on disk into standard,
 language-neutral file formats:
 HDF5 for dense or sparse matrices and tabular data,
 and JSON for object metadata.
 An R user can stage a 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SingleCellExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
MultiAssayExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 to a directory using 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
alabaster
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and a Python user can read that directory using 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
dolomite
\end_layout

\end_inset

 to reconstruct the corresponding BiocPy object (and vice versa).
 When objects are loaded in either R or Python,
 assay matrices are wrapped as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DelayedArray
\end_layout

\end_inset

 objects rather than read eagerly into memory,
 enabling out-of-core access to large datasets.
 This decouples on-disk data persistence from any single programming language.
 Consistency of the file representations is enforced by a validation library written in C++,
 ensuring that objects saved from either R or Python can be read faithfully in the other language (see the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "R"
target "https://github.com/biocpy/paper/blob/main/snippets/artifactdb_alabaster.R"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Python"
target "https://github.com/biocpy/paper/blob/main/snippets/artifactdb_dolomite.py"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 snippets).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Shared C++ Libraries as a Paradigm for Cross-Platform Method Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Even when two languages share identical data structures,
 independently reimplementing statistical methods in R and Python frequently introduces subtle algorithmic discrepancies
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "rich_impact_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Maintaining separate codebases also doubles the engineering effort required to fix bugs,
 optimize performance,
 and validate edge cases.
 To address this,
 computational packages in the BiocPy ecosystem follow a shared C++ core architecture (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Shared-cpp"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

